# Line endings are not a matter of taste here. A shell script that is checked out with CRLF
# fails under /bin/sh — the interpreter reads the carriage return as part of the command, and
# the script aborts before it creates anything. That failure appears in a container as an
# unhealthy database, which is a long way from the cause, so the rule below is a real control
# rather than cosmetic normalisation.
#
# This was not hypothetical: a test harness that rewrote the init script through a text-mode
# write on Windows converted it to CRLF, and the local stack stopped working while both the
# harness and the static test suite continued to report success.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything executed must keep LF regardless of the checkout configuration.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Windows tooling that genuinely requires CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never translated, never line-ending diffs on an image.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
